{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Visual Display.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Visual Display.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"visual display visual display","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Visual Display.md","fileName":"Visual Display.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Visual Display","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 13:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[XR Display Systems]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["XR Display Systems","Output Device","3D User Interface","Field of View","Field of Regard","Spatial Resolution","Refresh Rate","Depth Cue","Optical See-Through Display","Video See-Through Display","Spatial Augmented Reality","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Visual Display.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Visual Display.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"visual display visual display","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Visual Display.md","fileName":"Visual Display.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Visual Display","heading":"Visual Display","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A visual display is an [[Output Device]] that presents information through the human visual system. In a [[3D User Interface]], real-time graphics produce images that the device turns into perceptible light.\n\nImportant characteristics include [[Field of View]], [[Field of Regard]], [[Spatial Resolution]], screen geometry, light-transfer method, [[Refresh Rate]], ergonomics, and support for [[Depth Cue]]s. These characteristics interact with the task and technique. A front-projected screen, for example, can make direct manipulation difficult when the user's hand casts a shadow, while a wide display can make better use of peripheral vision.\n\nAn AR display can combine real and generated imagery through [[Optical See-Through Display|optical see-through]], [[Video See-Through Display|video see-through]], or [[Spatial Augmented Reality|spatial projection]]. These methods give the system different control over occlusion, brightness, focus, viewpoint, and the real image itself, so no one arrangement satisfies every application.","tags":["baby"],"links":["XR Display Systems","Output Device","3D User Interface","Field of View","Field of Regard","Spatial Resolution","Refresh Rate","Depth Cue","Optical See-Through Display","Video See-Through Display","Spatial Augmented Reality","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Visual Display.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Visual Display.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"visual display visual display","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Visual Display.md","fileName":"Visual Display.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Visual Display","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf]]\n[[augmentedreality_pearson.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["XR Display Systems","Output Device","3D User Interface","Field of View","Field of Regard","Spatial Resolution","Refresh Rate","Depth Cue","Optical See-Through Display","Video See-Through Display","Spatial Augmented Reality","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}